 ],
 "literal": "A male monkey's hand touched, fragrant-pulped big fruit, / guarding forgotten forest-man nearby, / in every guarded tree, catching-nets fastens, / O lord of the hill country! Is it fit? Fresh pool's / water lily's cool leaf-garment, she here to suffer, / of those who loved, the distress curing, / if you are of a character that does not yield benefit.",
 "translation": "O lord of the hills! The hunter, forgetting to guard, now fastens catching-nets on every guarded tree nearby to catch the male monkey that touched the fragrant-pulped big jackfruit. Is it fit for you to have a character that yields no benefit to cure the distress of those who love you, while she suffers here, wearing a cool garment of water-lily leaves from the fresh pool?",
 "modern": "ஆண் குரங்கின் கை தொட்ட, மணம் வீசும் சுளைகளைக் கொண்ட பெரிய பலாப்பழத்தைக் காவல் காக்க மறந்த கானவன், அதன் அருகில் உள்ள காவல் உடைய மரங்கள் தோறும் வலைகளை வீசி மாட்டுகிறான். குன்றுகளின் நாடனே! உன்னை விரும்பியவர்களின் துன்பத்தைத் தீர்க்கும் பயன் தராத பண்பினை உடையவன் நீ என்றால், புதிய சுனையில் மலர்ந்த குவளை மலர்களின் குளிர்ந்த தழையாடையை உடுத்திய இவள் இங்கே வருந்துவது தகுமோ?",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine's friend to the hero, urging him to marry the heroine quickly. She uses an implied metaphor (ullurai) from the mountain landscape. The hunter forgot to guard the sweet jackfruit, allowing a monkey to touch it; now, realizing his mistake, he sets nets on all the nearby trees to catch the monkey. This represents the heroine's family, who previously left her unguarded, allowing the hero to win her love, but have now placed her under strict confinement (irserippu) at home. The friend asks the hero if it is right for him to remain passive and yield no benefit (marriage) to cure the distress of the heroine who loves him, while she suffers in confinement.",
